# Cloudflare's Workers Static Assets serves everything under /_next/static/* (and other files in
# this public/ directory) directly through its own asset-serving path — Next.js's own
# next.config.ts `headers()` never runs for these, since that config only applies to requests that
# actually reach the Next.js/Worker request handler. This is the mechanism Cloudflare actually
# checks for static-asset responses (same "_headers" file convention as Cloudflare Pages).
#
# /_next/static/* holds content-hashed build output — a fresh filename every deploy, the exact
# same file's bytes never change once built — so it's safe to cache for a year. Without this it
# was being served as max-age=0, must-revalidate, forcing a revalidation request for every JS/CSS
# chunk on every single page view.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
